#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Deliberately inert on AstroOS. Replaces the CachyOS-Live-ISO script at the
# same (unowned) path, which has no safe translation; the overlay copy wins
# because container-build.sh copies astroos/overlay/airootfs over the base
# profile.
#
# What the replaced script did, and why none of it survives the port:
#
#   1. It built its removal list as
#        pacman -Qq  MINUS  ( pacman -Qqg base-devel
#                           + pacman -Qqg cachyos
#                           + two hardcoded keep lists )
#      and ran `pacman -Rs` over the result, i.e. "strip this system down to
#      base". The `cachyos` package GROUP does not exist on an AstroOS system,
#      so that subtraction keeps nothing from it and the list becomes nearly
#      every installed package: the desktop, the kernel, the network stack and
#      the installer included. There is no AstroOS group to put in its place,
#      so the computation cannot be ported, only reinvented, and a rewrite
#      that guesses at the keep list is exactly the failure mode being avoided.
#   2. Its `_try_v3` swapped /etc/pacman-more.conf in as /etc/pacman.conf on a
#      x86-64-v3 capable CPU. The installer does that itself now, in
#      shellprocess-before-online.conf, against the target root.
#   3. Its `_pacman_fix` ran `pacman-key --populate cachyos`. AstroOS trusts
#      one repository key, populated by shellprocess_initialize_pacman for the
#      target and by calamares-online.sh for the live session.
#
# Nothing calls it. Neither Calamares sequence names it (settings_online.conf
# and settings_offline.conf at the pinned installer commit), the live profile
# does not either, and upstream's own `removeun` deletes it from the target as
# a live leftover. AstroOS installs online with pacstrap into an empty root,
# so there is no live system to prune in the first place: the target only ever
# holds what the installer put there.
#
# The cleanup that IS wanted, for the offline path, lives in removeun.

cat >&2 <<'EOF'
removeun-online: not available on AstroOS.
  AstroOS installs online with pacstrap into an empty root, so there is no
  live system to strip. The replaced script computed its removal list against
  the "cachyos" package group, which does not exist here; that subtraction
  would have kept nothing and removed nearly every installed package.
  Offline-install cleanup lives in /usr/local/bin/removeun.
EOF
exit 1

# vim:set ft=bash sw=2 sts=2 et:
